# 5. Latest Release Commit ID — Merge Commit Inclusion (Deployment Section Only)

## Background

The application currently has a global rule to **exclude merge commits** during commit analysis. This rule is correct for Review Commits analysis. However, in the Deployment Section, the **Latest Release Commit ID** represents the absolute latest commit on the release branch — which may itself be a merge commit.

Kubernetes records the exact SHA at time of deployment, which can be a merge commit SHA. If the application excludes merge commits when fetching the latest release commit, there will be a mismatch.

## Problem Statement

The Latest Release Commit shown in the Deployment table is incorrect because the fetch logic is skipping the most recent commit if it is a merge commit. This causes a discrepancy between what Kubernetes deployed and what the application reports as the latest.

## ⚠️ Critical Isolation Rule

> **This change MUST NOT affect any other section.**  
> Review Commits analysis must continue to exclude merge commits.  
> Branch Cut Validation must continue to exclude merge commits.  
> Only the Latest Release Commit fetch within the Deployment Section is changed.

## Functional Requirements

| # | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-5.1 | When fetching the Latest Release Commit ID for the Deployment Section, retrieve the **absolute latest commit** regardless of whether it is a merge commit or a regular commit. |
| FR-5.2 | This behavior must be implemented as a **separate fetch path or parameter** — do NOT modify the shared commit fetch service in a way that affects other callers. |
| FR-5.3 | The merge commit inclusion flag must be scoped to the Deployment Section's fetch call only. |
| FR-5.4 | Review Commits, Branch Cut Validation, and Commits Behind calculation must continue using the existing merge-commit-excluded logic. |
| FR-5.5 | Add a clear code comment at the fetch call site explaining why merge commits are included here. |

## Acceptance Criteria

```
AC-5.1
GIVEN the latest commit on the release branch is a merge commit
WHEN the Deployment Section fetches the Latest Release Commit ID
THEN the merge commit SHA is returned and displayed as the Latest Release Commit ID

AC-5.2
GIVEN the latest commit on the release branch is a regular commit
WHEN the Deployment Section fetches the Latest Release Commit ID
THEN the regular commit SHA is returned and displayed correctly

AC-5.3
GIVEN the Deployment Section fetch now includes merge commits
WHEN the Review Commits section fetches commits
THEN merge commits are still excluded from Review Commits analysis

AC-5.4
GIVEN the Deployment Section fetch now includes merge commits
WHEN Branch Cut Validation runs
THEN merge commits are still excluded from that analysis

AC-5.5
GIVEN the fetch implementation is changed
WHEN a code review is performed
THEN a clear comment exists at the fetch call site indicating:
  "Merge commits included intentionally — Deployment Section requires absolute latest commit"

AC-5.6
GIVEN the Kubernetes commit SHA matches the latest merge commit on the release branch
WHEN the Deployment table renders
THEN both Kubernetes Commit ID and Latest Release Commit ID show identical SHAs
AND the status shows "Valid Deployment"
```

